---
title: The vesper command
menuLabel: Command line
summary: vesper agents writes instructions for coding agents, vesper manifest shows what Vesper reads from your site, and vesper deploy init writes a workflow that deploys it.
---

`vespercms` installs a `vesper` command. Run it with `npx` in your site's folder, or from a script in `package.json`. It reads your configs with your site's own Astro and Vite, so run it where your site's dependencies are installed.

## vesper agents

Writes instructions for coding agents, generated from your schema, between Vesper's markers in `AGENTS.md`. It creates the file if there isn't one, and leaves everything outside the markers as it was. [AI coding agents](/docs/agents/) explains what goes in it and how agents find it.

```sh
npx vesper agents
```

| Option | Default | |
|---|---|---|
| `--file <file>` | `AGENTS.md` | The file to write, relative to the site's folder. |
| `--check` | off | Write nothing. Exit with an error when the file is out of date, for CI. |
| `--root <folder>` | the current folder | The site's folder, for a site inside a larger repository. |
| `--src-dir <dir>` | `src` | Astro's `srcDir`, when the site changed it. |

It prints any problems it finds in your schema or config, the ones the studio would show you in development.

## vesper manifest

Prints the manifest: the JSON the studio is built from. It lists every collection with its fields as the studio sees them, URL patterns, page trees, Markdoc tags, declared MDX components, image ratios and accessibility settings. Use it to see how Vesper reads a schema, for example why a field shows as raw YAML.

```sh
npx vesper manifest
```

| Option | Default | |
|---|---|---|
| `--out <file>` | none | Write the manifest to a file instead of printing it. |
| `--root <folder>` | the current folder | The site's folder. |
| `--src-dir <dir>` | `src` | Astro's `srcDir`, when the site changed it. |

Problems with your schema or config go to standard error. The command exits with an error when one of them is an error rather than a warning.

`astro build` writes the same manifest to `dist/_vesper/manifest.json`. That file is how the cloud learns your site's schema without reading your repository's code.

## vesper deploy init

Writes a GitHub Actions workflow, `.github/workflows/vesper-deploy.yml` at your repository's root, that deploys the site. It's plain YAML that runs in your own GitHub account with your own secrets, and it's yours to read and change: Vesper never deploys a site itself.

```sh
npx vesper deploy init --target sftp
```

| Target | What the workflow does |
|---|---|
| `sftp` | Builds the site on every push to the default branch and copies Astro's output folder to your server by SFTP. |
| `ftp` | The same by FTP, over TLS only, since plain FTP sends the password as text. |
| `workers` | Builds the site on every push and deploys it to Cloudflare Workers with your site's own `wrangler`. |
| `hook` | For a host that already builds from your repository, such as Netlify, Cloudflare Pages or Vercel: on a schedule, when a scheduled change is due, it asks the host to build the site through its build hook. |

Every workflow also runs when the cloud starts it at a [scheduled time](/docs/site/scheduling/), and from the Actions tab. One deploy runs at a time, and a newer one waits for the one in progress rather than cancelling it.

| Option | Default | |
|---|---|---|
| `--target <target>` | none | `sftp`, `ftp`, `workers` or `hook`. |
| `--rebuild-every <minutes>` | none; 60 for `hook` | Also run on a schedule, and rebuild when a [scheduled change](/docs/site/scheduling/) is due: 5, 6, 10, 12, 15, 20, 30, 60, 120, 180, 240, 360, 480, 720 or 1440. |
| `--url <address>` | Astro's `site` | The live site's address, where a scheduled run reads `_vesper/schedule.json`. |
| `--branch <name>` | your repository's default branch | The branch whose pushes deploy. |
| `--root <folder>` | the current folder | The site's folder, for a site inside a larger repository. |
| `--force` | off | Replace a workflow that's already there. Without it, the command never does. |

It finds the rest in your repository: its root, your package manager and lockfile, a `.nvmrc` or `.node-version`, and Astro's output folder. Then it lists the secrets to add, under **Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions**:

| Target | Secrets |
|---|---|
| `sftp` | `VESPER_SFTP_HOST`, `VESPER_SFTP_USER`, `VESPER_SFTP_PATH` (the site's folder on the server), `VESPER_SFTP_KNOWN_HOSTS`, and `VESPER_SFTP_PASSWORD` or `VESPER_SFTP_KEY` (a private key); `VESPER_SFTP_PORT` when it isn't 22. |
| `ftp` | `VESPER_FTP_HOST`, `VESPER_FTP_USER`, `VESPER_FTP_PASSWORD`, `VESPER_FTP_PATH`; `VESPER_FTP_PORT` when it isn't 21. |
| `workers` | `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN`, from the "Edit Cloudflare Workers" template; `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` when your Wrangler config has no `account_id` and the token reaches more than one account. |
| `hook` | `VESPER_BUILD_HOOK`, the host's build hook address. |

A run with a secret missing fails before it uploads anything, and names the secret.

**Uploads delete what the build no longer has,** so an expired page leaves the server too. The folder in `VESPER_SFTP_PATH` or `VESPER_FTP_PATH` should hold only this site. The server's own `.well-known` and `cgi-bin` folders are left alone unless the build has its own.

**SFTP checks the server is the one you expect.** `VESPER_SFTP_KNOWN_HOSTS` holds the server's keys, from `ssh-keyscan` with the server's own port:

```sh
ssh-keyscan -p 22 sftp.example.com
```

The workflow refuses any other server, so a password or key never goes to anyone in between.
